#Teaser

A teaser for notlob, under thirty seconds and square. It follows one
real module from a real project -- Petri Marking, from the pn-chomper
game -- from its source on the page, to the claims notlob runs, out to
the name-graph of the whole project around it.

The scenes here are drawing only. What is shown comes from the other
modules: colours and frame from #Style, source listings from
#Highlight, where every name sits from #Graph, and the real results and
measurements from #Evidence. This module is where the beats of the
storyboard are built; for now it holds the stills and the first moving
beat, each a checkpoint before the beats are joined.

##Heritage

The first beat. Literate programming has a source, and the teaser
starts there: the last clause of the sentence in which Knuth, in 1984,
asked programmers to change their attitude to what a program is for.
Then one cut is made to it, on screen. Knuth's reader was a human
being; notlob's reader may also be an agent, and the sentence needs
only to lose three words to cover both. The attribution stays in view
throughout, so the cut reads as ours, made to his words. The clause
opens mid-sentence in the paper, with a small "let"; it is given a
capital here, as a quotation standing on its own usually is.

    QUOTE = ("Let us concentrate rather on explaining", "to human beings",
             "what we want a computer to do.")
    QUOTE_CUT = 1
    QUOTE_SOURCE = "Donald E. Knuth, Literate Programming, 1984"

What is left after the cut is still a sentence.

~example
    " ".join(q for i, q in enumerate(QUOTE) if i != QUOTE_CUT) == "Let us concentrate rather on explaining what we want a computer to do."

The beat's six seconds: the quotation appears and is given time to be
read -- not just once through, but long enough for the eye to settle
before anything changes -- then the cut is marked, deliberately, the
lines close over it, and the result holds.

    def heritage_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        return 0.8, 2.8, 0.6, 0.7, duration - 4.9

~example
    abs(sum(heritage_times(beat("heritage").duration)) - beat("heritage").duration) < 1e-9

The clause is set on three lines with the words to be cut alone on the
middle one, so that removing them is one clean move: a line through
them, then the outer lines closing the gap.

    def heritage_beat(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        lines = [sized_text(words, PROSE_FONT, PROSE, 0.24) for words in QUOTE]
        block = VGroup(*lines).arrange(DOWN, buff=0.3)
        source = sized_text("— " + QUOTE_SOURCE, PROSE_FONT, STRUCTURE, 0.15)
        source.next_to(block, DOWN, buff=0.7)
        fit_above_caption(VGroup(block, source))
        cut = lines[QUOTE_CUT]
        strike = Line(cut.get_left() + 0.12 * LEFT, cut.get_right() + 0.12 * RIGHT,
                      color=REF, stroke_width=3)
        close = (lines[QUOTE_CUT - 1].get_center()[1] - cut.get_center()[1]) / 2
        appear, read, mark, shut, hold = heritage_times(beat("heritage").duration)
        scene.play(FadeIn(block), FadeIn(source), FadeIn(caption(beat("heritage").caption)),
                   run_time=appear)
        scene.wait(read)
        scene.play(Create(strike), run_time=mark)
        scene.play(FadeOut(cut), FadeOut(strike),
                   *[line.animate.shift(close * DOWN) for line in lines[:QUOTE_CUT]],
                   *[line.animate.shift(close * UP) for line in lines[QUOTE_CUT + 1:]],
                   run_time=shut)
        scene.wait(hold - out)

    class HeritageBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            heritage_beat(self)

##The Star Module

Its opening -- an essay that cross-references the module it depends on,
then the code it describes -- is the first thing on screen.

    class ListingPreview(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            self.add(listing(STAR_MODULE, *OPENING))

Its claims -- three named properties and two examples, introduced by the
prose that says what they establish -- are the second still.

    class ClaimsPreview(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            self.add(listing(STAR_MODULE, 98, 148))

##One File

The second beat: what literate programming looks like in notlob. The
star module's opening is one file holding an essay and the code the
essay describes, each in its own face. Inside the essay is the one
piece of cyan on the page -- a cross-reference to another module, a
link that notlob checks -- and it is pointed out, because the last beat
will follow it.

    CROSS_REFERENCE = "#Petri Net"

The beat also makes the link the rest of the teaser depends on. To an
agent, a file is something read into its context window, and this one
arrives with its explanation attached. So the file is framed as a
context window and given its size in characters -- the unit the context
beat will measure in. The size is the whole module's, not only the part
on screen, so the frame is left open at the bottom, with an ellipsis,
to show the file running on.

    def one_file_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        return 0.8, 1.0, 0.8, 0.9, duration - 3.5

~example
    abs(sum(one_file_times(beat("one-file").duration)) - beat("one-file").duration) < 1e-9

The frame is three sides of a rectangle around the listing, with the
ellipsis where the fourth would be, and its labels along the top edge:
what it is on the left, the file and its measured size on the right.

    def context_frame(body, path: Path, project: Path) -> tuple:
        pad = 0.16
        left, right = body.get_left()[0] - pad, body.get_right()[0] + pad
        top, bottom = body.get_top()[1] + pad, body.get_bottom()[1] - pad
        frame = VMobject(stroke_color=PROSE, stroke_width=2)
        frame.set_points_as_corners([[left, bottom, 0], [left, top, 0], [right, top, 0],
                                     [right, bottom, 0]])
        more = VGroup(*[Dot(radius=0.03, color=PROSE) for _ in range(3)]).arrange(RIGHT, buff=0.1)
        more.move_to([(left + right) / 2, bottom, 0])
        name = sized_text("context window", CODE_FONT, PROSE, 0.12)
        name.move_to([left, top + 0.12, 0], aligned_edge=DL)
        size = sized_text(f"{path.relative_to(project).as_posix()}  ·  {file_context(path):,} characters",
                          CODE_FONT, STRUCTURE, 0.12)
        size.move_to([right, top + 0.12, 0], aligned_edge=DR)
        return frame, VGroup(more, name, size)

The cross-reference is found in the listing by its text, and the glyphs
that spell it are the ones that pulse.

    def one_file_beat(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        body, rows, (_, cap, _) = typeset_listing(STAR_MODULE, *OPENING)
        before = body.height
        fit_above_caption(body).scale(0.9).shift(0.12 * DOWN)
        cap *= body.height / before
        source = STAR_MODULE.read_text(encoding="utf-8").split("\n")
        line = next(n for n in sorted(rows) if CROSS_REFERENCE in source[n - 1])
        reference = name_glyphs(rows[line], source[line - 1], CROSS_REFERENCE, cap)
        frame, labels = context_frame(body, STAR_MODULE, STAR_PROJECT)
        appear, read, point, enclose, hold = one_file_times(beat("one-file").duration)
        scene.play(FadeIn(body), FadeIn(caption(beat("one-file").caption)), run_time=appear)
        scene.wait(read)
        scene.play(Indicate(reference, color=REF, scale_factor=1.6), run_time=point)
        scene.play(Create(frame), FadeIn(labels), run_time=enclose)
        scene.wait(hold - out)

    class OneFileBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            one_file_beat(self)

##Claims Run

The third beat. The star module's claims are on screen, and then the
results of actually running them arrive: a tick beside each line notlob
reports, in source order -- one per assertion for examples, one per
property at its sigil, exactly where notlob puts them. The ticks are not
drawn because the claims ought to pass; they are drawn because, run
moments before the render, they did.

    CLAIMS_EXCERPT = (105, 148)

A tick is two strokes, sized to the capitals of the code beside it.

    def tick(cap: float):
        mark = VMobject(color=CLAIM, stroke_width=2.5)
        mark.set_points_as_corners([[0, 0.4, 0], [0.32, 0.0, 0], [1, 1.0, 0]])
        return mark.scale_to_fit_height(cap * 1.1)

Ticks stand in the margin left of the claim bars, centred on the line
they belong to, in source order.

    def margin_ticks(rows: dict, grid: tuple, first: int, results: dict) -> list:
        advance, cap, row_pitch = grid
        x = -(CLAIM_BAR_GAP + 3.0) * advance
        ticks = []
        for line in sorted(results):
            if line in rows:
                mark = tick(cap)
                mark.move_to([x, -(line - 1 - first) * row_pitch + cap / 2, 0])
                ticks.append(mark)
        return ticks

A ticked listing fits the listing and its results to the frame
together.

    def ticked_listing(path: Path, first: int, last: int, results: dict) -> tuple:
        body, rows, grid = typeset_listing(path, first, last)
        ticks = margin_ticks(rows, grid, first, results)
        fit_above_caption(VGroup(body, *ticks))
        return body, ticks

Every claim in the excerpt gets at least one tick: each run of claim
lines has a result on one of its lines, or on the sigil just above it.

    def every_claim_ticked(source: str, results: dict, first: int, last: int) -> bool:
        claims = [n for n in claim_lines(source) if first <= n < last]
        return all(any(top <= line <= bottom + 1 for line in results)
                   for top, bottom in runs(claims))

The beat's time is the storyboard's: a moment for the listing to
appear, most of it for the ticks to land, the rest to hold.

    def claims_run_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        return 0.5, duration * 0.6, duration * 0.4 - 0.5

~example
    abs(sum(claims_run_times(beat("claims-run").duration)) - beat("claims-run").duration) < 1e-9

The beat is played onto whichever scene it is given, so it can be
rendered alone for review and later joined with the others.

    def claims_run(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        results = claim_results(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")
        body, ticks = ticked_listing(STAR_MODULE, *CLAIMS_EXCERPT, results)
        appear, land, hold = claims_run_times(beat("claims-run").duration)
        words = caption(beat("claims-run").caption)
        scene.play(FadeIn(body), FadeIn(words), run_time=appear)
        scene.play(LaggedStart(*[Create(t) for t in ticks], lag_ratio=0.6), run_time=land)
        scene.wait(hold - out)

    class ClaimsRunBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            claims_run(self)

##The Split

The fourth beat. One claim is seen to mean something. The star module's
second example states a split: a token in p1 fires through t1, and
afterwards p2 and p3 hold one each. In the game that is a chomper
walking through a doorway and becoming two. Above, the example; below,
the net it is about, drawn the way pn-chomper draws it; and the net,
both markings and the ticks all come from running pn-chomper's code.

    SPLIT_EXAMPLE = (142, 148)

The marking before firing places the chompers; the transition fired is
the one leading out of the room the chompers leave.

    def fired_transition(net: dict, before: dict, after: dict) -> str:
        emptied = {p for p, n in before.items() if n > after.get(p, 0)}
        return next(a["target"] for a in net["arcs"] if a["source"] in emptied)

~example
    fired_transition(SPLIT_NET, {"p1": 1}, {"p1": 0, "p2": 1, "p3": 1}) == "t1"

The beat's four seconds: the picture appears, the chomper walks to the
door, splits into the rooms beyond it, the ticks land, and it holds.

    def split_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        appear, walk, split = 0.5, 0.8, 0.8
        ticks = 1.0
        return appear, walk, split, ticks, duration - appear - walk - split - ticks

~example
    abs(sum(split_times(beat("split").duration)) - beat("split").duration) < 1e-9
    min(split_times(beat("split").duration)) > 0

The listing sits above the board, and the two are fitted to the frame
as one picture. Chompers are placed after fitting, sized to the rooms
they stand in. The board is drawn twice, for the marking before and the
marking after: before, the door can fire, so it is lit and its corridors
are gold; after, nothing can, and as the chompers arrive the board
turns into that quieter one.

    def split_beat(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        states = split_states(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")
        results = claim_results(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")
        first, last = SPLIT_EXAMPLE
        body, rows, grid = typeset_listing(STAR_MODULE, first, last)
        ticks = margin_ticks(rows, grid, first, results)
        listing_part = VGroup(body, *ticks)
        board, rooms, doors = draw_board(states, states["before"], show_labels=True)
        listing_part.scale_to_fit_width(board.width * 0.9)
        fit_above_caption(VGroup(listing_part, board).arrange(DOWN, buff=0.5))
        fired = draw_board(states, states["after"], show_labels=True)[0]
        fired.scale_to_fit_width(board.width).move_to(board)
        room_radius = rooms["p1"][0].width / 2
        size = room_radius * CHOMPER_RADIUS / PLACE_RADIUS
        walkers = [draw_chomper(size).move_to(rooms[p][0])
                   for p, n in sorted(states["before"].items()) for _ in range(n)]
        door = doors[fired_transition(states, states["before"], states["after"])][0]
        targets = [rooms[p][0].get_center()
                   for p, n in sorted(states["after"].items()) for _ in range(n)]
        appear, walk, split, land, hold = split_times(beat("split").duration)
        words = caption(beat("split").caption)
        scene.play(FadeIn(body), FadeIn(board), FadeIn(words), *[FadeIn(w) for w in walkers],
                   run_time=appear)
        scene.play(*[w.animate.move_to(door) for w in walkers], run_time=walk)
        arrivals = [walkers[0].copy() for _ in targets]
        scene.remove(*walkers)
        scene.play(*[a.animate.move_to(t) for a, t in zip(arrivals, targets)],
                   Transform(board, fired), run_time=split)
        scene.play(LaggedStart(*[Create(t) for t in ticks], lag_ratio=0.5), run_time=land)
        scene.wait(hold - out)

    class SplitBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            split_beat(self)

##Context

The fifth beat: why this matters to an agent, whose context window is
what it can afford to read. One question about pn-chomper, answered two
ways, each measured in characters actually taken in -- searching the
sources and reading the files that match, or asking notlob for the one
section that answers it. The bars share one scale, so their lengths are
the measurements; the counters climb to the measured values, and the
line beneath each bar says what was measured.

    def context_bars(search: int, query: int, full: float) -> tuple:
        return full, full * query / search

~example
    context_bars(40000, 2000, 6.0) == (6.0, 0.3)

The beat's time: the question appears, the search bar grows slowly and
long, the query bar quickly and short, and the difference holds.

    def context_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        return 0.5, 1.6, 0.6, duration - 2.7

Its hold is short on purpose: the next beat picks the query up again,
on the graph it was asked of.

~example
    abs(sum(context_times(beat("context").duration)) - beat("context").duration) < 1e-9

A row is a label and a climbing counter above a bar, with what was
measured beneath it in code type.

    LABEL_CAP = 0.2
    DETAIL_CAP = 0.13
    BAR_HEIGHT = 0.42

    def context_row(label: str, detail: str, colour: str, length: float, top: float,
                    left: float, right: float) -> tuple:
        title = sized_text(label, PROSE_FONT, PROSE, LABEL_CAP)
        title.move_to([left, top, 0], aligned_edge=UL)
        bar = Rectangle(width=max(length, 0.02), height=BAR_HEIGHT, fill_color=colour,
                        fill_opacity=1, stroke_width=0)
        bar.next_to(title, DOWN, buff=0.18, aligned_edge=LEFT)
        note = sized_text(detail, CODE_FONT, STRUCTURE, DETAIL_CAP)
        note.next_to(bar, DOWN, buff=0.16, aligned_edge=LEFT)
        count = ValueTracker(0)
        counter = always_redraw(lambda: sized_text(
            f"{int(count.get_value()):,} characters", CODE_FONT, colour, LABEL_CAP * 0.8
        ).move_to([right, title.get_center()[1], 0], aligned_edge=RIGHT))
        return VGroup(title, note), bar, counter, count

The two rows sit between the question at the top and the caption band
at the bottom, the second clearly apart from the first.

    def context_beat(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        search = search_context(STAR_PROJECT, SEARCH_WORD)
        query = query_context(STAR_PROJECT, ANSWER_ADDRESS)
        files = search_files(STAR_PROJECT, SEARCH_WORD)
        full = FRAME_UNITS - 2 * MARGIN - 0.4
        left, right = -full / 2, full / 2
        long, short = context_bars(search, query, full)
        question = sized_text(QUESTION, PROSE_FONT, PROSE, 0.26).move_to([0, 2.7, 0])
        first = context_row("Search and read", f'grep "{SEARCH_WORD}", then read {len(files)} files',
                            STRUCTURE, long, 1.6, left, right)
        second = context_row("Ask notlob", f'notlob query content "{ANSWER_ADDRESS}"',
                             REF, short, -0.3, left, right)
        appear, grow_search, grow_query, hold = context_times(beat("context").duration)
        words = caption(beat("context").caption)
        scene.play(FadeIn(question), FadeIn(first[0]), FadeIn(second[0]), FadeIn(words),
                   run_time=appear)
        scene.add(first[2], second[2])
        scene.play(GrowFromEdge(first[1], LEFT), first[3].animate.set_value(search),
                   run_time=grow_search)
        scene.play(GrowFromEdge(second[1], LEFT), second[3].animate.set_value(query),
                   run_time=grow_query)
        scene.wait(hold - out)

    class ContextBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            context_beat(self)

##The Whole Project

The name-graph of all of pn-chomper, laid out by #Graph: each module a
flower of its own names, foundations in the middle, the binding around
the rim. Nodes are coloured by kind and edges weighted by kind, so the
same palette that coloured the source now colours its structure.

    STAR_GRAPH, STAR_LAYOUT = project_layout(STAR_PROJECT)
    NODE_RADIUS = 0.22

    def node_dots(graph: dict, laid) -> dict:
        kinds = {n["address"]: n["kind"] for n in graph["nodes"]}
        return {
            address: Dot([x, y, 0], radius=NODE_RADIUS,
                         color=NODE_COLOURS.get(kinds[address], STRUCTURE))
            for address, (x, y) in laid.positions.items()
        }

    def edge_lines(graph: dict, laid) -> list:
        return [
            Line([*laid.positions[e["source"]], 0], [*laid.positions[e["target"]], 0],
                 color=EDGE_COLOURS[e["kind"]], stroke_width=EDGE_WIDTH[e["kind"]],
                 stroke_opacity=EDGE_OPACITY[e["kind"]])
            for e in graph["edges"]
        ]

##The Graph

The last beat. The star module's names leave the page: a dot appears at
each line of the listing that defines a name and flies to its place in
the module's flower. Its prose cross-reference to Petri Net is drawn as
an edge heading out of view, and then the view pulls back to show where
it goes -- and everything else the module is part of.

    STAR_CLUSTER = "petri/marking"
    OPENING = (0, 41)

The edge the story follows is the one the listing shows as a
cross-reference in the module's opening essay.

    def hero_edge(graph: dict) -> int:
        return next(i for i, e in enumerate(graph["edges"])
                    if (e["kind"], e["source"], e["target"])
                    == ("REFERENCES", STAR_CLUSTER, "petri/net"))

The names that lift off are those of the star module defined on a line
the listing is showing.

    def liftable(graph: dict, laid, rows: dict) -> list:
        return [n["address"] for n in graph["nodes"]
                if laid.cluster_of[n["address"]] == STAR_CLUSTER and n.get("start_line") in rows]

Each name that lifts off takes its own text with it: a copy of the
very glyphs that spell it in the listing, so the name is seen to leave
the line it was written on. If a name cannot be found in its line, it
is set afresh in code type instead.

    def name_glyphs(row, text: str, label: str, cap: float):
        start = text.find(label)
        glyphs, index = visible_glyphs(row), glyph_indices(text)
        if start < 0 or len(glyphs) != len(index):
            return sized_text(label, CODE_FONT, CODE, cap).move_to(row.get_left(), aligned_edge=LEFT)
        return VGroup(*[glyphs[index[p]].copy()
                        for p in range(start, start + len(label)) if p in index])

A name in the graph is more than its text: it has an address, the
module's address and then the name. That is the form the query in the
beat before used, and it is what makes the graph something that can be
asked questions. So as each name flies it becomes its address -- the
name's own glyphs take their place at the end, and the module's part
appears in front of them. Addresses are set level, reading away from
the flower, beside the dots they belong to. A node whose address does not end in its
label, the module itself, simply turns into its address.

    def address_text(address: str, label: str, colour: str, cap: float, point, direction):
        full = sized_text(address, CODE_FONT, STRUCTURE, cap).next_to(point, direction, buff=0.2)
        glyphs = visible_glyphs(full)
        count = len(label.replace(" ", "")) if address.endswith(label) else 0
        prefix, tail = VGroup(*glyphs[:len(glyphs) - count]), VGroup(*glyphs[len(glyphs) - count:])
        if count:
            tail.set_color(colour)
        else:
            prefix.set_color(colour)
        return prefix, tail

The question from the beat before had an answer at one address. That
address is a node here, a subheading of the star module, and what the
query returned is that node and what it holds.

    def query_nodes(graph: dict) -> list:
        parents = tree_parents(graph)
        return [ANSWER_ADDRESS] + sorted(a for a, p in parents.items() if p == ANSWER_ADDRESS)

What the query touched is not a passing flash. Those nodes turn the
colour of the query and grow, the edges among them with them, and they
stay that way as the view pulls back -- so the answer is still there to
be seen, a small lit part of the whole graph.

    ASKED_SCALE = 1.5

    def mark_asked(dot) -> None:
        dot.set_color(REF).scale(ASKED_SCALE)

    def mark_among(line) -> None:
        line.set_stroke(color=REF, opacity=0.9, width=2.5)

The beat's time: the listing, the lift-off, the query, the reference,
the pull back, and the end card. The lift-off has three parts of its
own: names and dots appear on their lines, fly to the flower becoming
addresses as the listing fades, and the rest of the flower arrives.

    def graph_times(duration: float) -> tuple:
        return 0.6, 1.5, 1.3, 0.5, 1.4, duration - 5.3

    LIFT_PARTS = (0.2, 0.5, 0.3)

~example
    abs(sum(graph_times(beat("graph").duration)) - beat("graph").duration) < 1e-9
    graph_times(beat("graph").duration)[-1] > 0

The view does not use a moving camera, which would carry the caption
band away with it. The graph itself is moved instead: it starts scaled
up so the star module's flower fills the picture, and is then shrunk and
re-centred to the whole project. What lies outside the flower starts
invisible and appears as the view widens. The close-up sits left of
centre: the names that lift off are the first on the flower's ring, up
and to the right, and their addresses need the room beside them.

    CLOSE_UP_RADIUS = 2.0
    CLOSE_UP_CENTRE = (-1.2, CAPTION_BAND / 2)

    def close_up(whole, laid) -> None:
        x, y, radius = laid.clusters[STAR_CLUSTER]
        scale = CLOSE_UP_RADIUS / radius
        whole.scale(scale, about_point=[0, 0, 0])
        whole.shift([CLOSE_UP_CENTRE[0] - x * scale, CLOSE_UP_CENTRE[1] - y * scale, 0])

When the view has pulled back, the graph is named for what it is: the
output of a command, with its real size.

    def graph_summary(graph: dict) -> str:
        return f"notlob graph  ->  {len(graph['nodes'])} nodes, {len(graph['edges'])} edges"

~example
    graph_summary({"nodes": [1, 2], "edges": [3]}) == "notlob graph  ->  2 nodes, 1 edges"

The beat itself follows: the pieces are prepared in their final places,
then moved back to where each one starts.

    def graph_beat(scene, out: float = 0.0):
        graph, laid = STAR_GRAPH, STAR_LAYOUT
        dots = node_dots(graph, laid)
        edges = edge_lines(graph, laid)
        hero = hero_edge(graph)
        edges[hero].set_stroke(color=REF, width=3.5, opacity=1)
        whole = VGroup(*edges, *dots.values())
        whole.target = fit_above_caption(whole.copy()).scale(0.9).shift(0.15 * DOWN)
        asked = [a for a in query_nodes(graph) if a in dots]
        among = [i for i, e in enumerate(graph["edges"])
                 if e["source"] in asked and e["target"] in asked]
        order = list(dots)
        for a in asked:
            mark_asked(whole.target[len(edges) + order.index(a)])
        for i in among:
            mark_among(whole.target[i])
        close_up(whole, laid)
        star = {a for a in dots if laid.cluster_of[a] == STAR_CLUSTER}
        inner = [edges[i] for i, e in enumerate(graph["edges"])
                 if e["source"] in star and e["target"] in star]
        for i, e in enumerate(graph["edges"]):
            if not (e["source"] in star and e["target"] in star):
                edges[i].set_stroke(opacity=0)
        for address, dot in dots.items():
            if address not in star:
                dot.set_opacity(0)

        body, rows, (_, cap, _) = typeset_listing(STAR_MODULE, *OPENING)
        before = body.height
        fit_above_caption(body)
        cap *= body.height / before
        source = STAR_MODULE.read_text(encoding="utf-8").split("\n")
        nodes = {n["address"]: n for n in graph["nodes"]}
        lifted = liftable(graph, laid, rows)
        homes = {a: dots[a].get_center() for a in lifted}
        centre = homes.get(STAR_CLUSTER, dots[STAR_CLUSTER].get_center())
        names = {}
        for a in lifted:
            line = nodes[a]["start_line"]
            names[a] = name_glyphs(rows[line], source[line - 1], nodes[a]["label"], cap)
            dots[a].move_to(names[a].get_left() + 0.2 * LEFT)
        settled = [dots[a] for a in sorted(star) if a not in lifted]

        flights, labels = [], []
        for a in lifted:
            away = homes[a] - centre
            outward = UP if np.linalg.norm(away) < 1e-6 else (RIGHT if away[0] >= 0 else LEFT)
            colour = NODE_COLOURS.get(nodes[a]["kind"], STRUCTURE)
            prefix, tail = address_text(a, nodes[a]["label"], colour, cap * 1.25, homes[a], outward)
            flights.append(dots[a].animate.move_to(homes[a]))
            if len(tail):
                flights += [Transform(names[a], tail), FadeIn(prefix)]
                labels += [names[a], prefix]
            else:
                flights.append(Transform(names[a], prefix))
                labels.append(names[a])

        command = VGroup(
            sized_text("notlob query content", CODE_FONT, STRUCTURE, 0.13),
            sized_text(f'"{ANSWER_ADDRESS}"', CODE_FONT, REF, 0.13),
        ).arrange(RIGHT, buff=0.12, aligned_edge=DOWN)
        command.move_to([-FRAME_UNITS / 2 + MARGIN, FRAME_UNITS / 2 - MARGIN, 0], aligned_edge=UL)
        summary = sized_text(graph_summary(graph), CODE_FONT, STRUCTURE, 0.13)
        summary.move_to([-FRAME_UNITS / 2 + MARGIN, FRAME_UNITS / 2 - MARGIN, 0], aligned_edge=UL)
        words = caption(beat("graph").caption)

        show, lift, query, refer, pull, end = graph_times(beat("graph").duration)
        appear, fly, settle = (lift * part for part in LIFT_PARTS)
        scene.play(FadeIn(body), FadeIn(words), run_time=show)
        scene.play(*[FadeIn(dots[a]) for a in lifted], *[FadeIn(names[a]) for a in lifted],
                   run_time=appear)
        scene.play(*flights, FadeOut(body), run_time=fly)
        scene.play(*[FadeIn(m) for m in inner + settled], run_time=settle)
        scene.play(FadeIn(command), run_time=query * 0.25)
        scene.play(*[dots[a].animate.set_color(REF).scale(ASKED_SCALE) for a in asked],
                   *[edges[i].animate.set_stroke(color=REF, opacity=0.9, width=2.5) for i in among],
                   run_time=query * 0.35)
        scene.wait(query * 0.4)
        scene.add(whole)
        edges[hero].set_stroke(opacity=1)
        scene.play(Create(edges[hero]), *[FadeOut(m) for m in labels], FadeOut(command),
                   run_time=refer)
        scene.play(MoveToTarget(whole), run_time=pull * 0.75)
        scene.play(FadeIn(summary), run_time=pull * 0.25)
        scene.play(FadeOut(words), FadeIn(caption(END_CARD)), run_time=end / 2)
        scene.wait(end / 2 - out)

    class GraphBeat(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            graph_beat(self)

The whole project, still, for checking the layout at a glance.

    class GraphPreview(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            edges = edge_lines(STAR_GRAPH, STAR_LAYOUT)
            self.add(fit_to_frame(VGroup(*edges, *node_dots(STAR_GRAPH, STAR_LAYOUT).values())))

##Sounds

What is heard, and when. The voices and the mixing come from #Sound;
this section says which sound goes with which moment. Each beat's
sounds are written from the same timing function its pictures use, so
a sound cannot drift from the thing it accompanies -- move a beat's
timing and its sounds move with it.

Ticks, and anything else that arrives one after another, are drawn with
a staggered start. The moment each one begins follows from how many
there are, how far they overlap and how long the whole takes.

    def lagged_starts(count: int, lag_ratio: float, run_time: float) -> list:
        if count == 0:
            return []
        each = run_time / (1 + (count - 1) * lag_ratio)
        return [i * lag_ratio * each for i in range(count)]

~example
    lagged_starts(3, 0.5, 2.0) == [0.0, 0.5, 1.0]
    lagged_starts(0, 0.5, 2.0) == []

Three numbers come from the real material and decide how many sounds
there are: the ticks in the claims excerpt, the ticks in the split's
example, and the names that lift off the opening listing.

    def result_count(results: dict, first: int, last: int) -> int:
        return len([line for line in results if first < line <= last])

    def sound_counts() -> dict:
        results = claim_results(STAR_PROJECT, "petri/marking.lob")
        rows = typeset_listing(STAR_MODULE, *OPENING)[1]
        return {
            "claims": result_count(results, *CLAIMS_EXCERPT),
            "split": result_count(results, *SPLIT_EXAMPLE),
            "lifted": len(liftable(STAR_GRAPH, STAR_LAYOUT, rows)),
        }

Heritage: a low note as the quotation appears, a knock where the cut is
struck, two rising notes as the lines close over it.

    def heritage_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        appear, read, mark, shut, _ = heritage_times(duration)
        cut = appear + read
        return [(0.0, "marimba", 0, 0.5), (cut, "knock", 0, 0.5),
                (cut + mark, "marimba", 3, 0.4), (cut + mark + shut / 2, "marimba", 5, 0.45)]

One file: a note as the listing appears, a brighter one for the
cross-reference, and three rising notes for the three sides of the
context window's frame.

    def one_file_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        appear, read, point, enclose, _ = one_file_times(duration)
        frame = appear + read + point
        return [(0.0, "marimba", 7, 0.4), (appear + read, "marimba", 12, 0.45),
                *[(frame + k * enclose / 3, "marimba", step, 0.35)
                  for k, step in enumerate((5, 7, 8))]]

Claims run: a woodblock for every tick, climbing the scale.

    def claims_run_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        appear, land, _ = claims_run_times(duration)
        return [(0.0, "marimba", 5, 0.35),
                *[(appear + start, "woodblock", 10 + i % 10, 0.4)
                  for i, start in enumerate(lagged_starts(counts["claims"], 0.6, land))]]

The split: one note as the chomper sets off, one at the door, then two
together as it arrives as two -- and the ticks.

    def split_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        appear, walk, split, land, _ = split_times(duration)
        arrive = appear + walk + split
        return [(appear, "marimba", 5, 0.4), (appear + walk, "marimba", 8, 0.45),
                (arrive - split * 0.1, "marimba", 10, 0.4), (arrive - split * 0.1, "marimba", 12, 0.4),
                *[(arrive + start, "woodblock", 12 + i, 0.4)
                  for i, start in enumerate(lagged_starts(counts["split"], 0.5, land))]]

Context: the comparison is made audible. The search bar grows to a long
roll of rising notes; the query bar gets two.

    ROLL_STEP = 0.1

    def context_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        appear, grow_search, _, _ = context_times(duration)
        roll = [(appear + i * ROLL_STEP, "marimba", 3 + i, 0.18 + 0.01 * i)
                for i in range(int(grow_search / ROLL_STEP))]
        asked = appear + grow_search
        return [(0.0, "marimba", 5, 0.35), *roll,
                (asked, "marimba", 10, 0.45), (asked + 0.15, "marimba", 12, 0.45)]

The graph: a pluck for each name that lifts, a chord when the query
lights its nodes, a note for the cross-reference, a falling run as the
view pulls back, and a low octave on the end card.

    def graph_sounds(duration: float, counts: dict) -> list:
        show, lift, query, refer, pull, _ = graph_times(duration)
        lifted = max(counts["lifted"], 1)
        lit = show + lift + query * 0.25
        edge = show + lift + query
        back = edge + refer
        return [(0.0, "marimba", 5, 0.35),
                *[(show + lift * 0.2 + i * lift * 0.5 / lifted, "marimba", 7 + i, 0.35)
                  for i in range(counts["lifted"])],
                *[(lit, "marimba", step, 0.3) for step in (5, 7, 8, 10)],
                (edge, "marimba", 12, 0.45),
                *[(back + i * pull * 0.75 / 5, "marimba", 14 - 2 * i, 0.3) for i in range(5)],
                (back + pull, "marimba", 0, 0.5), (back + pull, "marimba", 5, 0.5)]

Every beat has its sounds, and they are placed on the storyboard's
timeline.

    BEAT_SOUNDS = {
        "heritage": heritage_sounds,
        "one-file": one_file_sounds,
        "claims-run": claims_run_sounds,
        "split": split_sounds,
        "context": context_sounds,
        "graph": graph_sounds,
    }

    def teaser_sounds(counts: dict) -> list:
        return sorted((start + time, voice, step, gain)
                      for start, played in timeline(BEATS)
                      for time, voice, step, gain in BEAT_SOUNDS[played.name](played.duration, counts))

~example
    [b.name for b in BEATS] == list(BEAT_SOUNDS)

The track is mixed to the storyboard's length and written beside the
other rendered output.

For now the teaser is rendered silent. The first track, pitched notes on
a pentatonic scale, sounded like a children's programme against pictures
that are not one. The events and their timing are kept, since a quieter,
less melodic set of voices could reuse them; only the switch is off.

    SOUND_ON = False
    SOUND_FILE = Path("../media/sound/teaser.wav")

    def teaser_track() -> Path:
        length = sum(played.duration for played in BEATS)
        return write_wav(mix(teaser_sounds(sound_counts()), length), SOUND_FILE)

##The Whole Teaser

The beats, joined in the storyboard's order. Every beat of the
storyboard has a function here that plays it.

    BEAT_SCENES = {
        "heritage": heritage_beat,
        "one-file": one_file_beat,
        "claims-run": claims_run,
        "split": split_beat,
        "context": context_beat,
        "graph": graph_beat,
    }

~example
    [b.name for b in BEATS] == list(BEAT_SCENES)

Between beats the stage fades to the background. The fade is not extra
time: a beat that is followed by another gives up that much of its
final hold, so the whole teaser lasts exactly what the storyboard says
and stays within its limit. Every such beat has hold enough to give.

    FADE = 0.4

~example
    heritage_times(beat("heritage").duration)[-1] > FADE
    one_file_times(beat("one-file").duration)[-1] > FADE
    claims_run_times(beat("claims-run").duration)[-1] > FADE
    split_times(beat("split").duration)[-1] > FADE
    context_times(beat("context").duration)[-1] > FADE

Clearing the stage stops anything still updating itself -- the context
beat's counters -- before fading everything out.

    def clear_stage(scene, fade: float) -> None:
        for mobject in scene.mobjects:
            mobject.clear_updaters()
        scene.play(*[FadeOut(mobject) for mobject in scene.mobjects], run_time=fade)
        scene.clear()

    class WholeTeaser(Scene):
        def construct(self):
            if SOUND_ON:
                self.add_sound(str(teaser_track().resolve()))
            names = [b.name for b in BEATS]
            for name in names[:-1]:
                BEAT_SCENES[name](self, FADE)
                clear_stage(self, FADE)
            BEAT_SCENES[names[-1]](self)

##Rendering

Frame, colours and output location all come from #Style. The whole
teaser is rendered, and each beat alone for review.

~run
    render_square(WholeTeaser)
    render_square(HeritageBeat)
    render_square(OneFileBeat)
    render_square(ContextBeat)
    render_square(SplitBeat)
    render_square(ClaimsRunBeat)
    render_square(GraphBeat)
    render_still(GraphPreview)
    render_still(ListingPreview)
    render_still(ClaimsPreview)

